# AOS configuration.
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is git-ignored and MUST NEVER be
# committed. No secret in this repository is real — this file lists names and
# explains what each value is, and nothing else.
#
# Every variable here is read by a Node process (Backend/*.mjs), not by the
# browser. Anything the browser reads is prefixed VITE_ and is therefore
# PUBLIC — never put a secret behind a VITE_ name.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Operational database (Backend/migrate.mjs, Backend/api-server.mjs)
#
# The authoritative store for customers, cases, requirements, document
# METADATA, verification, submissions, users and the event log. Document BYTES
# do not live here — they stay on disk under AOS_STORAGE_ROOT below, and the
# database only ever holds the storage path (see document.file_path's column
# comment in Database/migrations/0005).
#
# In the office one PC runs Postgres and the AOS backends; the other PCs point
# their browsers at it. AOS_DB_HOST is 127.0.0.1 on that server PC.
#
# These are read by Node processes only. Nothing here is prefixed VITE_, so no
# connection detail can ever reach the browser bundle.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

AOS_DB_HOST=127.0.0.1
AOS_DB_PORT=5432
AOS_DB_NAME=aos

# WHICH ROLE THE APPLICATION CONNECTS AS.
#
# `postgres` is a SUPERUSER. Connecting as it means a bug or an injection in
# AOS can run programs on the server PC, read any file the PostgreSQL service
# can read, read every role's password hash, and erase the audit log — none of
# which AOS needs. It also means row level security does nothing at all, since
# a superuser bypasses it unconditionally.
#
# Migration 0033 creates `aos_app` for this: no superuser, no RLS bypass, owns
# nothing, and holds no DELETE privilege on any table. Set its password once
# (Docs/Installation.md §5a) and put it here. `postgres` is only the starting
# value because a fresh install has no other role yet.
AOS_DB_USER=postgres

# The administrative scripts — `npm run seed-users`, `npm run
# bootstrap-production` — deliberately cannot run as `aos_app`: it is not
# allowed to create accounts or append to the event log outside an
# authenticated request, which is exactly what a bootstrap is. Set these when
# AOS_DB_USER becomes `aos_app`, so those two scripts keep using the owner
# account while the running application does not. Leave them unset otherwise
# and the scripts share the ordinary connection.
# AOS_DB_ADMIN_USER=postgres
# AOS_DB_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# The application API (Backend/api-server.ts). It is the only process that
# talks to the database. Employees do not reach it directly — the web server
# below proxies /api to it over loopback.
AOS_API_PORT=4321

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NETWORK BINDING — the setting that decides whether the office can use AOS
#
# READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING EITHER VALUE.
#
# Every AOS process defaults to 127.0.0.1 (loopback), which means "this machine
# only". That is correct for a development checkout and for an employee's PC,
# and it is why a fresh install is safe by default.
#
# THE OFFICE SERVER PC — and only that one machine — sets:
#
#     AOS_WEB_HOST=0.0.0.0
#
# which makes the built frontend and the API reachable from other PCs at
# http://<server LAN IP>:<AOS_WEB_PORT>. Employees bookmark that address and
# run nothing themselves (Docs/Deployment Topology.md).
#
# DO NOT set this on an employee's PC. A second machine running the backend
# gets its own empty database and its own document store on its own disk;
# uploads made through it are invisible to everyone else and nothing warns
# that it has happened.
#
# AOS_API_HOST exists for the case where something else (a reverse proxy, a
# TLS terminator) fronts the API instead of the bundled web server. Leave it
# at the default unless you are doing that: the web server reaches the API
# over loopback regardless, so there is no reason to open a second port.
#
# The storage backend (4319) and the mail backend (4320) CANNOT be moved off
# loopback. Both refuse to start if asked. Neither has any authentication:
# anything that can reach the storage server can read every customer's
# documents, and anything that can reach the mail server can send email as
# Amaze Loans. The API is the front door because the API checks permissions.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# What employees put in their browser. 0.0.0.0 on the office server ONLY.
AOS_WEB_HOST=127.0.0.1
AOS_WEB_PORT=4300

# Leave as-is unless a reverse proxy fronts the API instead of the web server.
AOS_API_HOST=127.0.0.1

# Which browser origins may read API responses (comma-separated). Leave unset
# and the API allows the bundled web server on AOS_WEB_PORT plus Vite's 5173,
# which covers the office and development. Any other origin is refused the
# CORS header entirely.
#
# Employees reaching AOS through the bundled web server do NOT need this: that
# server proxies /api on its own origin, so the browser sends no Origin header
# at all. Set it only if a reverse proxy or a hostname fronts AOS, e.g.
# AOS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://aos.office.local:4300
#
# CORS is enforced by browsers and by nothing else — it limits what a web page
# an employee visits can do, never what an authenticated request may do. Every
# route checks the token and the permission regardless.
# AOS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Session lifetime
#
# TWO INDEPENDENT LIMITS, and a session ends when it hits either.
#
#   AOS_SESSION_TTL_MS   ABSOLUTE lifetime. A session reaches this and ends
#                        however busy the employee has been — the ceiling
#                        exists precisely so activity cannot extend a session
#                        forever. Default 12 hours: a workday.
#
#   AOS_SESSION_IDLE_MS  INACTIVITY timeout. Measured from the last
#                        authenticated request (api_session.last_seen_at,
#                        touched on every one). Default 2 hours: longer than a
#                        lunch break and a site visit, shorter than an evening.
#
# Both are checked SERVER-SIDE on every request. The browser also holds its
# token in sessionStorage rather than localStorage, so closing the browser ends
# the session in the browser too — but that is a convenience, not the control.
# Neither limit depends on the browser cooperating, and neither uses
# `beforeunload`, which fires unreliably and is not a security mechanism.
#
# The right numbers are an office policy, not a property of the code. Shorten
# them on a shared machine; do not lengthen them past a working day.
# AOS_SESSION_TTL_MS=43200000
# AOS_SESSION_IDLE_MS=7200000

# Set at install time. Never commit a real value — .env is git-ignored and this
# example file must keep the placeholder.
AOS_DB_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Document storage (existing — Backend/storage-server.mjs)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where document bytes live on this machine. Defaults to C:\AOS\Data.
AOS_STORAGE_ROOT=C:\AOS\Data
AOS_STORAGE_PORT=4319

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backup (Backend/backup.mjs, Backend/restore.mjs)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where `npm run backup` writes one timestamped folder per run, each holding
# a full database dump (aos.dump) and a copy of the document tree. Put this
# on a different disk (or a mapped network drive) from AOS_STORAGE_ROOT — a
# backup that lives on the same disk it protects survives a full disk but not
# much else.
AOS_BACKUP_ROOT=C:\AOS\Backups

# How many backup runs `npm run backup` keeps before deleting the oldest.
# Older ones are only ever removed AFTER a new backup succeeds.
AOS_BACKUP_RETENTION=14

# The PostgreSQL 'bin' folder holding pg_dump.exe / pg_restore.exe / psql.exe.
# Only needed if it isn't at the Windows installer's default location
# (C:\Program Files\PostgreSQL\17\bin) or on PATH.
# AOS_PG_BIN_DIR=

# Restoring onto a database whose name matches AOS_DB_NAME (the office
# database) requires this to equal that name — see Backend/restore.mjs. Not
# meant to be set in .env; export it for the one command that needs it.
# AOS_RESTORE_CONFIRM=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email (Backend/mail-server.mjs)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# unconfigured | gmail | capture
#
#   unconfigured  Default. Every send is refused with a clear message. Nothing
#                 is queued and nothing is faked.
#   gmail         Real delivery. Requires the four AOS_GMAIL_* values below.
#   capture       TEST ONLY. Writes messages to disk and reports success.
#                 The Playwright suite sets this. Never set it on an office
#                 install — the timeline would record submissions that never
#                 left the building.
AOS_MAIL_PROVIDER=unconfigured
AOS_MAIL_PORT=4320

# The mailbox AOS sends as. This is a display default, not an authorisation:
# Gmail sends as whichever account the refresh token belongs to, whatever is
# put on the From line. AOS never fakes a From header.
AOS_MAIL_SENDER_ADDRESS=amazeloans@gmail.com
AOS_MAIL_SENDER_NAME=Amaze Loans

# --- Gmail OAuth2 ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHY OAUTH2 AND NOT A PASSWORD: Google stopped accepting account passwords
# from applications in 2022, and AOS must not store one in any case.
#
# WHY A REFRESH TOKEN AND NOT A SERVICE ACCOUNT: a service account with
# domain-wide delegation can only impersonate users in a Google WORKSPACE
# domain. amazeloans@gmail.com is a consumer account, so delegation is not
# available to it. If Amaze moves to Workspace on its own domain, the service
# account route becomes the better option and only Backend/mail-server.mjs
# changes.
#
# HOW TO OBTAIN THESE — done once, by an administrator, signed in as the
# sending mailbox:
#
#   1. console.cloud.google.com → create (or pick) a project.
#   2. APIs & Services → Library → enable "Gmail API".
#   3. APIs & Services → OAuth consent screen → External, add
#      amazeloans@gmail.com as a Test user. (An unverified app's refresh
#      tokens expire after 7 days while the app is in "Testing"; move it to
#      "In production" — no Google review is needed when the only users are
#      your own test users — or the office will be reconnecting weekly.)
#   4. Credentials → Create credentials → OAuth client ID → Desktop app.
#      That gives the client id and client secret below.
#   5. Authorise once with scope https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.send
#      (the SEND-ONLY scope — AOS never reads the mailbox) and exchange the
#      resulting code for a refresh token. Google's OAuth Playground does this
#      with "Use your own OAuth credentials" ticked.
#
# The refresh token is a long-lived credential for the Amaze Loans mailbox.
# Treat it exactly as you would the password: never commit it, never paste it
# into a ticket, and revoke it at myaccount.google.com/permissions if it leaks.
AOS_GMAIL_CLIENT_ID=
AOS_GMAIL_CLIENT_SECRET=
AOS_GMAIL_REFRESH_TOKEN=

# How long to wait for Gmail before giving up on one email.
AOS_MAIL_TIMEOUT_MS=60000

# capture provider only — where captured messages are written.
# AOS_MAIL_CAPTURE_DIR=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WhatsApp — NOT IMPLEMENTED
#
# The provider seam exists (src/domain/communications/whatsapp-provider.ts)
# and nothing implements it. These names are recorded so that whoever does has
# the list in front of them; setting them today has no effect.
#
# Obtaining them is not a developer task: it needs a verified Meta Business
# account for Amaze Loans Pvt Ltd, a WhatsApp Business Account with a
# dedicated phone number (which then stops working in the handset app), and
# message templates approved by Meta for each workflow. It also needs a
# PUBLIC HTTPS webhook, which this installation does not have — the local
# backends bind to 127.0.0.1.
#
# See Docs/Email and WhatsApp Integration.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# AOS_WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
# AOS_WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# AOS_WHATSAPP_BUSINESS_ACCOUNT_ID=
# AOS_WHATSAPP_WEBHOOK_VERIFY_TOKEN=
